<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John II, Count Palatine of Simmern</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1492">1492</a>–<a href="/years/1557">1557</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a> and Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a> from 1509 to 1557, ruling a small Wittelsbach territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a> together with a share of Sponheim. He introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in his lands and was a patron of learning, supporting a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press at Simmern. His son Frederick III inherited the Electoral Palatinate in 1559, when the senior electoral line died out.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-ii-count-palatine-of-simmern.jpg" alt="John II, Count Palatine of Simmern" width="233" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JohannIISimmern.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John II was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a> on 21 March 1492 into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a>, the eldest surviving son of John I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a> of Simmern, and Countess Joanna of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a>, a daughter of Johann II of Nassau-Saarbrücken. In 1508 he married Beatrix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, daughter of Margrave Christoph I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>John succeeded his father as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a> in 1509 and held the county until his death in 1557. He allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> to be established in Simmern and was a patron of sculpture. His introduction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> into Simmern raised tensions with his neighbours, the Archbishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>.</p><p>With Beatrix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> he had twelve children, born between 1510 and 1532. Their eldest son, Frederick III, was born on 14 February 1515 and lived until 1576; Georg died in 1569 and Reichard in 1598, while William died in infancy in 1527. Several daughters entered religious life: Katherine became abbess of the Kumbd monastery, Joanna abbess of Marienberg near Boppard, where her sister Ottilie was a nun, and Brigitte abbess of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a> monastery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamoral-count-of-egmont">Lamoral, Count of Egmont</a>, and Elizabeth, Maria and Helena also lived to adulthood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After nineteen years as a widower John married the thirty-one-year-old Countess Maria Jakobine of Oettingen, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a>, Count of Oettingen; this marriage remained childless, and after John's death she married Count Johann of Schwarzenberg-Hohenlandsberg. John died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a> on 18 May 1557, aged 65, and was buried with both his wives in the Wittelsbach family vault of Saint Stephen's Church at Simmern.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John II, Count Palatine of Simmern” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern/Hunsrück</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern/Hunsrück</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.98,7.52&amp;year=1534" aria-label="Simmern/Hunsrück on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John II, Count Palatine of Simmern"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5412 5829h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5412 5829h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.98,7.52&amp;year=1534">What was here in 1534? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
